<p>Michelle Beer, professor of philosophy at <a href="https://case.fiu.edu/philosophy/">Florida International University,</a> has died.<span id="more-56668"></span></p>
<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="wp-image-56670 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/beer-michelle-950x1024.jpg" alt="" width="401" height="432" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/beer-michelle-950x1024.jpg 950w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/beer-michelle-278x300.jpg 278w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/beer-michelle-768x828.jpg 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/beer-michelle-400x431.jpg 400w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/beer-michelle.jpg 999w" sizes="(max-width: 401px) 100vw, 401px" /></p>
<p>Professor Beer taught at FIU from 1982 until her retirement in December 2025. A specialist in metaphysics and the philosophy of science, she was well-known for her article “<a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/2219921">Temporal Indexicals and the Passage of Time</a>,” first published in <em>The Philosophical Quarterly</em> in 1988 and later reprinted in <em>The New Theory of Time</em>, edited by L. Nathan Oaklander and Quentin Smith. You can learn about her other writings <a href="https://philpeople.org/profiles/michelle-beer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p>
<p>Before receiving her Ph.D. in Philosophy from the <a href="https://www.pitt.edu/">University of Pittsburgh</a>, she was an undergraduate in Physics at <a href="https://www.brown.edu/">Brown</a> and a master’s student in Physics at <a href="https://www.yu.edu/">Yeshiva University</a>, where she studied with Peter Bergmann, who himself was a student of Einstein. An ardent defender of the B-theory, her interest in the philosophy of time was informed by her background in physics.</p>
<p>At FIU, she taught a wide variety of courses, principally in her areas of specialization, but also in feminist philosophy. A vegetarian from an early age, she championed animal welfare throughout her life.</p>
